ML_PAGE("t1-subjective", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l1\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">TOPIC 1 · SUBJECTIVE EXAM PREPARATION</span><h2>Writing Answers That Earn Marks</h2><p>Define, explain the mechanism, apply it to the case, evaluate the trade-off, recommend. The answer technique and model answers are below.</p></header><div class=\"theme-l1\"><div class=\"card accent-card mb-20\">\n    <h3>The 5-move ERM answer (5-15 mark questions)</h3>\n    <div class=\"flow-grid\">\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>1. Define</b><p>State the concept precisely, e.g. risk as the effect of uncertainty on objectives.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>2. Explain the mechanism</b><p>Why it works that way: exposure → event → consequence.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>3. Apply to the case</b><p>Name the objective, the exposure and the risk in the scenario's own facts.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>4. Evaluate</b><p>Compare options or classifications; show the trade-off.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>5. Recommend</b><p>Pick a response and justify it against the objective.</p></div>\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"card-note\">Marks rise with Bloom's level: recognition earns little; application, analysis and justified decisions earn most.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"compare-box span-all\">\n    <div class=\"compare-side\">\n        <h4>Weak answer</h4>\n        <p>\"The company faces liquidity risk.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-weak\">Recognition only: no cause, no mechanism, no link to objectives, no action.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"compare-side highlight\">\n        <h4>Strong answer</h4>\n        <p>\"Customer payment delays have reduced cash inflows, creating funding liquidity pressure despite substantial fixed assets. Management should strengthen receivables collection and cash reserves to meet upcoming obligations.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-good\">Cause + risk type + mechanism + consequence + recommendation.</p>\n    </div>\n</div>\n<h3 class=\"block-title\">Quick model-answer bank</h3>\n<div class=\"scenario-grid\">\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">DEFINE</span>\n        <h4>Define risk in ERM and explain why objectives come first. (5 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-t1-1\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-t1-1\">Risk is the effect of uncertainty on objectives; the effect may be positive, negative or a deviation from what was expected. Objectives come first because the same event affects organisations differently: higher oil prices threaten an airline's profit objective but raise an oil producer's revenue. Without a clear objective there is nothing to measure the effect against, so relevant risks cannot be identified or prioritised.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">COMPARE</span>\n        <h4>Differentiate risk and uncertainty with examples. (6 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-t1-2\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-t1-2\">Under risk, the possible outcomes are known in advance and their probabilities may be estimated, e.g. a bank's loan-default rate from historical data. Under uncertainty, even the possible outcomes may be unknown, let alone their odds, e.g. launching a disruptive technology whose users, regulation and failure modes cannot be foreseen. Risk is therefore more quantifiable; uncertainty requires judgement, scenarios and flexibility.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">APPLY</span>\n        <h4>Explain avoidance, loss prevention and loss reduction with one example each. (9 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-t1-3\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-t1-3\">Avoidance never acquires an exposure or abandons an existing one, reducing the chance of loss to zero, e.g. declining to enter a high-risk country, at the cost of forgoing its upside. Loss prevention reduces the frequency of loss, e.g. safety features on hazardous products. Loss reduction reduces severity after a loss occurs, e.g. automatic sprinklers limit fire damage. Prevention acts on probability; reduction acts on impact.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">ANALYSE</span>\n        <h4>Explain why diversification removes some risks but not others. (10 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-t1-4\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-t1-4\">Total risk = systematic + unsystematic risk. Unsystematic (firm-specific) events such as lawsuits, strikes or a CEO's resignation are not perfectly correlated across firms, so in a large portfolio they partly cancel and are essentially eliminated. Systematic risk arises from economy-wide factors (business cycles, inflation, interest rates) that affect all assets together, so it remains however many assets are held. Diversification only works if assets do not all react to the same drivers. Hence investors are compensated for systematic risk, measured by beta.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">CASE</span>\n        <h4>A single-supplier manufacturer is hit by floods at the supplier, delays orders, faces online complaints and has property but little cash. Identify four risks and how they connect. (11 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-t1-5\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-t1-5\">Resource risk: dependence on one supplier for critical materials. Operational risk: the shortage stops production. Business/customer risk: unfulfilled orders and lost sales. Reputational risk: public dissatisfaction damages the brand. Funding liquidity risk: property-rich but cash-poor, it may not meet short-term obligations. Chain: supplier disruption → production stop → lost revenue and reputation → cash shortfall. Responses: diversify suppliers (prevention), hold safety stock and a liquidity buffer (reduction).</div>\n    </div>\n</div>\n</div><h3 class=\"block-title\">Practice questions from the notes</h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P1</span><span>Define risk in the context of Enterprise Risk Management. <small class=\"muted\">(5 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Define risk in the context of Enterprise Risk Management. Explain why establishing organisational objectives is essential when identifying risk.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Risk, in the context of Enterprise Risk Management, can be defined as the effect of uncertainty on organisational objectives. This effect may be positive, negative or a deviation from expected outcomes.</p>\n<p>Establishing organisational objectives is essential because risk cannot be meaningfully evaluated without identifying what the organisation intends to achieve.</p>\n<p>For example, an unexpected increase in crude-oil prices may increase operating costs for an airline, threatening its profitability objective. However, the same event may create a revenue opportunity for an oil producer.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the consequences of an uncertain event depend on the organisation's objectives and exposure.</p>\n<p>Consequently, organisations must establish clear objectives before identifying, assessing and managing relevant risks.</p>\n<p>This follows the broader risk definition used on page 8 of your lecture.</p>\n<h3>Suggested marking breakdown</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Correct definition of risk</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain positive/negative deviation</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain connection with objectives</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relevant organisational example</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Appropriate conclusion</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Why this answer is stronger</h3>\n<p>A weak answer would be:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Risk is the possibility of experiencing loss.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is a traditional definition, but it misses the more comprehensive ERM interpretation.</p>\n<p>The stronger answer demonstrates:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Risk}=\\text{Uncertainty affecting objectives}}$$</div>\n<p>and explains why the same event can affect different organisations differently.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P2</span><span>Differentiate between risk and uncertainty. <small class=\"muted\">(6 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Differentiate between risk and uncertainty. Provide appropriate business examples to illustrate your answer.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Risk and uncertainty are related concepts, but they differ in the degree of information available about possible outcomes.</p>\n<p>Risk refers to a situation in which potential outcomes can be identified and the likelihood of these outcomes may be estimated.</p>\n<p>For example, a bank may use historical borrower data to estimate the probability that a customer will default on a loan. Although repayment is not guaranteed, the bank can assess the risk using available information.</p>\n<p>Uncertainty, on the other hand, refers to situations where the possible outcomes, their probabilities or both are not adequately known.</p>\n<p>For example, a company introducing a completely new technology may struggle to anticipate consumer behaviour, future regulations and unexpected market developments because historical information is limited.</p>\n<p>The distinction is important because measurable risks are generally more suitable for quantitative assessment, whereas conditions involving substantial uncertainty may require scenario analysis, expert judgement and adaptive decision-making.</p>\n<p>Therefore, although risk and uncertainty both concern the future, risk is generally more structured and measurable than uncertainty.</p>\n<p>The lecture introduces the distinction using knowledge of possible outcomes and their odds.</p>\n<h3>Comparison table for revision</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>Risk</th>\n<th>Uncertainty</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Possible outcomes</td>\n<td>Relatively identifiable</td>\n<td>May be difficult to identify</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Probability</td>\n<td>May be estimated</td>\n<td>Often difficult to estimate reliably</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Measurement</td>\n<td>More feasible</td>\n<td>More challenging</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Example</td>\n<td>Historical loan-default exposure</td>\n<td>Disruptive new technology</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Management approach</td>\n<td>Quantitative assessment where appropriate</td>\n<td>Scenarios, judgement and adaptation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Higher-order insight</h3>\n<p>Suppose a company estimates:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(\\text{machine failure})=5\\\\%$$</div>\n<p>That is a quantifiable risk estimate.</p>\n<p>However, suppose the company faces an entirely new regulatory environment with no meaningful historical precedent.</p>\n<p>Assigning a precise probability such as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(\\text{regulatory disruption})=17.43\\\\%$$</div>\n<p>would not necessarily make the estimate scientifically credible.</p>\n<p>Precision is not the same as accuracy.</p>\n<p>An advanced subjective answer should recognise the limitations of available information rather than pretending that every uncertainty can be reliably quantified.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P3</span><span>Define loss exposure. <small class=\"muted\">(8 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Define loss exposure. Explain how loss frequency and loss severity are used to assess risks, using a manufacturing company as an example.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Loss exposure refers to any situation or circumstance in which a loss is possible, regardless of whether an actual loss occurs.</p>\n<p>For example, a manufacturing facility that stores flammable chemicals faces fire-related loss exposure even if it has never experienced a fire.</p>\n<p>Risk managers commonly assess such exposures using two important dimensions: loss frequency and loss severity.</p>\n<p>Loss frequency refers to the probable number of losses occurring within a specified period. For example, a factory may experience several minor workplace accidents annually.</p>\n<p>Loss severity refers to the probable magnitude or financial impact of each loss. A major factory fire may be infrequent but could cause significant damage to equipment, inventory and production facilities.</p>\n<p>Both dimensions are necessary because a frequent low-severity event and an infrequent catastrophic event require different management responses.</p>\n<p>For minor recurring accidents, management may prioritise preventive safety training to reduce frequency.</p>\n<p>For a potentially catastrophic fire, management may install automatic sprinklers and prepare recovery arrangements to reduce severity.</p>\n<p>Therefore, evaluating both frequency and severity enables management to prioritise exposures and allocate resources more appropriately.</p>\n<p>These concepts are introduced on pages 13-14 of the lecture.</p>\n<h3>Suggested marking breakdown</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Loss exposure definition</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loss frequency explanation</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loss severity explanation</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relevant application and management implications</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P4</span><span>Differentiate between risk avoidance, loss prevention and loss reduction. <small class=\"muted\">(9 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Differentiate between risk avoidance, loss prevention and loss reduction. Evaluate how each technique may be applied by a manufacturing company.</p>\n<p>This is one of the most examinable questions because the three techniques appear very similar.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Risk control refers to techniques used to reduce the frequency or severity of losses. The three principal techniques discussed in the lecture are risk avoidance, loss prevention and loss reduction.</p>\n<p>Risk avoidance involves not undertaking an activity that creates a particular loss exposure, or discontinuing an existing exposure.</p>\n<p>For example, a chemical manufacturer may decide not to produce an extremely hazardous product because the potential consequences exceed its acceptable risk level.</p>\n<p>Avoidance removes exposure arising from that specific activity. However, it may also eliminate potential business opportunities and revenue.</p>\n<p>Loss prevention involves implementing measures to reduce the probability or frequency of a loss occurring.</p>\n<p>For example, a manufacturer may introduce employee safety training, regular equipment inspection and preventive maintenance to reduce workplace accidents or machinery failures.</p>\n<p>Loss reduction involves measures that reduce the consequences or severity of a loss after an event occurs.</p>\n<p>For example, installing automatic fire sprinklers may limit physical damage if a factory fire occurs.</p>\n<p>In evaluating these techniques, management should consider the likelihood and severity of the exposure, implementation costs and the importance of the activity to business objectives.</p>\n<p>For commercially essential activities, prevention and reduction may be more appropriate than complete avoidance. However, avoidance may be justified when potential losses are unacceptable or cannot be adequately controlled.</p>\n<p>The lecture defines these three techniques on pages 15-16.</p>\n<h3>The comparison you should remember</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>Objective</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>Main limitation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Avoidance</td>\n<td>Remove the exposure</td>\n<td>Stop hazardous activity</td>\n<td>May sacrifice business opportunities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loss prevention</td>\n<td>Reduce frequency</td>\n<td>Safety training</td>\n<td>Event may still occur</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loss reduction</td>\n<td>Reduce severity</td>\n<td>Fire sprinklers</td>\n<td>Damage may still occur</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Examiner's trap</h3>\n<p>Suppose management installs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>safety sensors that stop machinery before accidents,</li>\n<li>fire sprinklers that operate after ignition.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both are safety systems, but their intended effects differ.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Safety sensors → Loss prevention\"><span>Safety sensors</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Loss prevention</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Fire sprinklers → Loss reduction\"><span>Fire sprinklers</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Loss reduction</span></div>\n<p>Always classify the technique by its intended effect, not by the equipment's name.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P5</span><span>Distinguish between pure risk and speculative risk. <small class=\"muted\">(6 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Distinguish between pure risk and speculative risk. Explain why the distinction is relevant when selecting risk-management techniques.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Pure risk refers to a situation in which the possible outcomes are either loss or no loss, without an opportunity for financial gain.</p>\n<p>Examples include accidental fires, workplace injuries and natural disasters.</p>\n<p>Speculative risk, on the other hand, involves the possibility of either financial gain or financial loss.</p>\n<p>For example, a company investing in shares or expanding into a new market may generate positive returns or suffer losses.</p>\n<p>The distinction is important because the appropriate risk-management response may differ.</p>\n<p>Pure risks such as fire and accidental property damage can often be managed using loss prevention, loss reduction and insurance.</p>\n<p>Speculative risks generally require evaluation of potential rewards against possible losses. Depending on the exposure, management may use diversification, hedging or strategic analysis.</p>\n<p>Therefore, pure risks are primarily associated with loss exposures, whereas speculative risks require management to consider both opportunities and threats.</p>\n<p>The distinction and examples follow pages 18-19 of the lecture.</p>\n<h3>What makes this a good 6-mark answer?</h3>\n<p>It does not merely define the two risks. It explains why the difference matters to management.</p>\n<p>That final management implication is often what separates a basic answer from a more developed one.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P6</span><span>Differentiate between diversifiable and non-diversifiable risks. <small class=\"muted\">(10 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Differentiate between diversifiable and non-diversifiable risks. Explain how diversification affects total portfolio risk and discuss the implications for investors.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Diversifiable risk, also known as unsystematic or firm-specific risk, refers to risk associated with individual companies or particular industries rather than the entire market.</p>\n<p>Examples include company-specific lawsuits, labour strikes, unsuccessful product launches and management failures.</p>\n<p>These risks can be reduced substantially through diversification because different companies may experience different positive and negative outcomes.</p>\n<p>For example, an investor holding shares in only one automobile manufacturer may suffer significant losses if the company experiences a labour strike.</p>\n<p>However, an investor holding shares across multiple unrelated industries is less dependent on any individual firm's performance.</p>\n<p>Non-diversifiable risk, also called systematic or market risk, refers to risk arising from broad economic or market factors affecting many companies simultaneously.</p>\n<p>Examples include inflation, changes in interest rates, recessions and business cycles.</p>\n<p>Unlike firm-specific risks, systematic risks cannot be eliminated simply by holding a large number of securities because many assets may respond to the same economic event.</p>\n<p>Conceptually, total portfolio risk consists of systematic and unsystematic components.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\text{Total Risk} = \\text{Systematic Risk} + \\text{Unsystematic Risk} }$$</div>\n<p>As the portfolio becomes more diversified, unsystematic risk generally decreases because firm-specific outcomes partly offset one another.</p>\n<p>However, systematic risk remains.</p>\n<p>Therefore, investors should diversify their holdings to reduce avoidable firm-specific exposure while recognising that broader market risks may still affect the portfolio.</p>\n<p>These distinctions are directly covered by the lecture's discussion of diversifiable and non-diversifiable risks and the concept of diversification.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P7</span><span>Explain the relationship between beta, systematic risk and expected return. <small class=\"muted\">(6 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Explain the relationship between beta, systematic risk and expected return. Why is systematic risk particularly relevant to diversified investors?</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Beta is a measure of an asset's relative systematic risk, reflecting its sensitivity to broad market movements.</p>\n<p>According to the lecture, an asset with a beta greater than 1 has above-average systematic risk, while an asset with a beta below 1 has below-average systematic risk.</p>\n<p>For example, a share with a beta of 1.5 has greater systematic market sensitivity than a share with a beta of 0.7.</p>\n<p>Systematic risk is especially important to diversified investors because diversification can substantially reduce unsystematic risk but cannot eliminate market-wide risk.</p>\n<p>Consequently, under standard asset-pricing theory, investors generally require higher expected returns for exposure to greater systematic risk, assuming other relevant conditions remain unchanged.</p>\n<p>However, a higher beta does not guarantee that an investment will actually deliver a higher realised return.</p>\n<p>Therefore, beta is useful for assessing the systematic-risk component of an asset rather than measuring every source of investment risk.</p>\n<p>The lecture emphasises the role of beta and the relationship between systematic risk and expected returns on page 25.</p>\n<h3>Possible examiner trap</h3>\n<p>If asked:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Does an investment with a beta of 1.8 definitely earn a higher return than one with a beta of 0.8?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The answer is no.</p>\n<p>A higher beta is associated with a higher required or expected return in standard asset-pricing models, not a guaranteed actual return.</p>\n<p>That's a useful evaluation-level distinction.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P8</span><span>Identify and explain five major categories of enterprise risk. <small class=\"muted\">(10 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Identify and explain five major categories of enterprise risk. Illustrate how each may affect the achievement of organisational objectives.</p>\n<p>There are several valid combinations of risk categories. The following answer uses five categories explicitly discussed in the chapter.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Enterprise risk encompasses major risks capable of affecting an organisation's operations, financial performance, reputation and continued existence.</p>\n<p>Five important categories are:</p>\n<p>1. Operational risk</p>\n<p>Operational risk arises from failures involving internal processes, people, technology or control systems.</p>\n<p>For example, an unexpected IT-system failure may prevent a company from processing customer transactions, resulting in lost revenue and dissatisfaction.</p>\n<p>2. Credit risk</p>\n<p>Credit risk refers to the possibility that a debtor or counterparty fails to make required payments or makes them late.</p>\n<p>For example, if customers default on credit sales, the organisation may experience financial losses and reduced cash inflows.</p>\n<p>3. Liquidity risk</p>\n<p>Liquidity risk concerns an organisation's ability to obtain sufficient cash or liquid resources to meet financial obligations when required.</p>\n<p>For example, a company may own substantial property assets but struggle to pay suppliers because it cannot obtain immediate cash.</p>\n<p>4. Market risk</p>\n<p>Market risk arises from movements in market variables or the prices of financial instruments, such as interest rates, exchange rates and security prices.</p>\n<p>For example, unfavourable foreign-exchange movements may increase the cost of imported materials.</p>\n<p>5. Reputational risk</p>\n<p>Reputational risk arises when stakeholders develop negative perceptions of an organisation because their expectations have not been met.</p>\n<p>For example, a major product-safety scandal may reduce customer confidence and future sales.</p>\n<p>These risks are interconnected. An operational failure may generate customer dissatisfaction, reputational damage and financial losses.</p>\n<p>Therefore, effective enterprise risk management requires organisations to understand both individual risk categories and their potential interactions.</p>\n<p>The lecture covers these categories across pages 28-35.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P9</span><span>Differentiate between credit risk and liquidity risk. <small class=\"muted\">(8 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Differentiate between credit risk and liquidity risk. Further distinguish between asset liquidity risk and funding liquidity risk.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Credit risk refers to the possibility that a borrower or counterparty fails to fulfil payment obligations, resulting in late payment or financial loss to the creditor.</p>\n<p>For example, a bank may experience credit risk when a borrower defaults on a loan.</p>\n<p>Liquidity risk, on the other hand, refers to difficulties in obtaining the cash required to fulfil financial obligations or execute transactions when necessary.</p>\n<p>Liquidity risk can be divided into two categories.</p>\n<p>Asset liquidity risk refers to difficulty purchasing or selling an asset because there are insufficient market participants or limited trading activity.</p>\n<p>For example, a company may be unable to sell specialised equipment quickly without accepting a substantial price reduction.</p>\n<p>Funding liquidity risk refers to the inability to meet short-term payment obligations as they become due.</p>\n<p>For example, a profitable company may lack sufficient immediately available cash to meet payroll or repay debt.</p>\n<p>Credit risk and liquidity risk can be interconnected. Customer defaults may reduce cash inflows and subsequently cause funding difficulties.</p>\n<p>Therefore, credit risk primarily concerns counterparty repayment, while liquidity risk concerns availability of cash or the ability to transact when needed.</p>\n<p>The lecture distinguishes credit and liquidity risks on pages 30-31.</p>\n<h3>The easiest memory technique</h3>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Credit Risk}=\\text{They cannot pay me}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Funding Liquidity Risk}=\\text{I cannot pay now}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Asset Liquidity Risk}=\\text{I cannot sell easily}}$$</div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P10</span><span>A Malaysian manufacturing company operates a single production facility that produces electronic components. <small class=\"muted\">(15 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A Malaysian manufacturing company operates a single production facility that produces electronic components.</p>\n<p>The company depends heavily on one overseas supplier for critical raw materials. Recently, severe flooding disrupted the supplier's operations, causing a shortage of materials. Production was delayed, customer orders could not be fulfilled, and several customers began expressing dissatisfaction online.</p>\n<p>Meanwhile, the company has substantial property assets but insufficient cash to meet short-term obligations.</p>\n<p>Required:</p>\n<p>(a) Identify and explain FOUR risks faced by the company. (8 marks)</p>\n<p>(b) Explain how these risks are interconnected. (3 marks)</p>\n<p>(c) Recommend TWO appropriate risk-management responses and justify them. (4 marks)</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h2>Model answer - Part (a), 8 marks</h2>\n<p>1. Resource risk</p>\n<p>The company faces resource risk because it depends heavily on a single supplier for critical raw materials.</p>\n<p>The supplier's disruption prevents the company from obtaining necessary production inputs, threatening its ability to fulfil customer orders.</p>\n<p>2. Operational risk</p>\n<p>The shortage of raw materials interrupts manufacturing activities.</p>\n<p>As the factory cannot operate at its intended capacity, production schedules are delayed, reducing operational efficiency and output.</p>\n<p>3. Reputational risk</p>\n<p>Customers become dissatisfied when orders are delayed.</p>\n<p>Negative customer feedback may weaken confidence in the company, potentially affecting future customer relationships and sales.</p>\n<p>4. Funding liquidity risk</p>\n<p>Although the company owns substantial property assets, it does not have enough immediately available cash to meet short-term financial obligations.</p>\n<p>Therefore, it faces funding liquidity risk despite having valuable assets.</p>\n<h2>Model answer - Part (b), 3 marks</h2>\n<p>The company's risks are interconnected because one event produces several consequences across the organisation.</p>\n<p>The flood causes supplier disruption, creating a shortage of raw materials.</p>\n<p>This leads to production delays, which prevent the company from fulfilling orders.</p>\n<p>Customer dissatisfaction then increases reputational exposure, while delayed revenue receipts worsen the firm's liquidity position.</p>\n<h3>Risk transmission chain</h3>\n<p>Flood disrupts supplier</p>\n<p>External event</p>\n<p>Raw materials unavailable</p>\n<p>Resource risk</p>\n<p>Manufacturing delayed</p>\n<p>Operational consequence</p>\n<p>Customer dissatisfaction</p>\n<p>Reputational risk</p>\n<p>Delayed cash inflows</p>\n<p>Funding liquidity pressure</p>\n<p>Threat to profitability and business continuity</p>\n<p>This demonstrates why evaluating only the original flood risk would underestimate the event's total organisational impact.</p>\n<h2>Model answer - Part (c), 4 marks</h2>\n<p>Recommendation 1: Diversify critical suppliers</p>\n<p>Management should identify and qualify additional suppliers rather than depending entirely on a single source.</p>\n<p>This could reduce the probability that one supplier's failure causes a complete interruption of production.</p>\n<p>However, supplier diversification may increase procurement and coordination costs, which should be evaluated against the expected reduction in disruption risk.</p>\n<p>Recommendation 2: Establish business-continuity and liquidity arrangements</p>\n<p>Management should develop contingency arrangements such as maintaining appropriate safety stocks, establishing alternative production arrangements and maintaining adequate liquid reserves or committed financing.</p>\n<p>These measures may reduce the severity of production disruptions and financial losses if another supplier failure occurs.</p>\n<p>However, they also involve holding costs, financing costs and other resource commitments.</p>\n<p>Consequently, management should select controls based on their effectiveness and cost relative to the potential losses.</p>\n<h3>Why this answer can score well</h3>\n<p>The answer does not simply identify:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Resource, operational, reputation, liquidity.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Instead, it demonstrates the full causal sequence:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Event → Exposure → Risk Consequence → Objective Affected → Management Response\"><span>Event</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Exposure</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Risk Consequence</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Objective Affected</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Management Response</span></div>\n<p>That is the difference between remembering and analysing.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P11</span><span>Two companies operate in the same industry. <small class=\"muted\">(12 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Two companies operate in the same industry.</p>\n<p>Company A experiences frequent minor equipment failures, each resulting in relatively small losses.</p>\n<p>Company B faces a very low probability of a catastrophic incident that could destroy its primary production facility and threaten the survival of the organisation.</p>\n<p>Both companies estimate similar average annual financial losses from their respective exposures.</p>\n<p>Evaluate whether both risks should receive the same management priority. Recommend appropriate risk-control techniques for each company.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Although both companies estimate similar average annual financial losses, their risks should not automatically receive the same management priority because their loss-frequency and loss-severity characteristics differ.</p>\n<p>Company A faces a high-frequency, relatively low-severity exposure.</p>\n<p>Repeated equipment failures may create recurring maintenance expenses, production interruptions and reduced operational efficiency.</p>\n<p>The most suitable response may involve loss prevention, such as regular equipment inspections, preventive maintenance and employee training.</p>\n<p>These measures seek to reduce the frequency of failures.</p>\n<p>Company B faces a low-frequency but extremely high-severity exposure.</p>\n<p>Although the catastrophic incident is unlikely, its consequences could include destruction of assets, prolonged operational disruption, major financial loss and possible business failure.</p>\n<p>Management should therefore place considerable emphasis on measures that reduce the severity of the incident, such as automatic protection systems, contingency arrangements and business-continuity planning.</p>\n<p>Loss prevention should also be considered where the probability of the incident can be reduced.</p>\n<p>The two exposures should not be assessed solely using average expected loss. Company B's catastrophic risk may be more important because a single event could exceed its financial capacity to recover.</p>\n<p>Consequently, prioritisation should consider loss frequency, severity, consequences for organisational objectives and the ability of each firm to absorb losses.</p>\n<h3>Higher-level conclusion</h3>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Equal expected losses do not necessarily imply equal risk-management decisions.</span></div>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Because:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The distribution of losses can differ.</li>\n<li>One exposure may threaten organisational survival.</li>\n<li>Different controls may be cost-effective.</li>\n<li>Financial resilience may differ.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These are analytical considerations extending the lecture's frequency/severity framework.</p></div></details></div></details></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l1\" id=\"t1s-toc\"><summary><strong>Notes cover</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">17 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"Part 1 - How to construct a high-scoring subjective answer\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1s-s1\" title=\"Part 1 - How to construct a high-scoring subjective answer\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P1</span>Building a high-scoring answer</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 2 - The fundamental concept of risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1s-s2\" title=\"Part 2 - The fundamental concept of risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P2</span>The fundamental concept of risk</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 3 - Risk vs uncertainty\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1s-s3\" title=\"Part 3 - Risk vs uncertainty\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P3</span>Risk vs uncertainty</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 4 - Loss exposure, frequency and severity\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1s-s4\" title=\"Part 4 - Loss exposure, frequency and severity\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P4</span>Loss exposure, frequency and severity</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 5 - Risk-control techniques\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1s-s5\" title=\"Part 5 - Risk-control techniques\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P5</span>Risk-control techniques</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 6 - Pure risk vs speculative risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1s-s6\" title=\"Part 6 - Pure risk vs speculative risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P6</span>Pure risk vs speculative risk</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 7 - Diversifiable vs non-diversifiable risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1s-s7\" title=\"Part 7 - Diversifiable vs non-diversifiable risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P7</span>Diversifiable vs non-diversifiable risk</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 8 - Beta and compensation for risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1s-s8\" title=\"Part 8 - Beta and compensation for risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P8</span>Beta and compensation for risk</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 9 - Enterprise risk categories\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1s-s9\" title=\"Part 9 - Enterprise risk categories\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P9</span>Enterprise risk categories</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 10 - Credit risk vs liquidity risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1s-s10\" title=\"Part 10 - Credit risk vs liquidity risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P10</span>Credit risk vs liquidity risk</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 11 - A complete 15-mark case study\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1s-s11\" title=\"Part 11 - A complete 15-mark case study\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P11</span>A complete 15-mark case study</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 12 - A more challenging evaluation question\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1s-s12\" title=\"Part 12 - A more challenging evaluation question\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P12</span>A more challenging evaluation question</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 13 - Tricky terminology that can cost marks\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1s-s13\" title=\"Part 13 - Tricky terminology that can cost marks\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P13</span>Tricky terminology that can cost marks</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 14 - Additional exam questions to practise independently\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1s-s14\" title=\"Part 14 - Additional exam questions to practise independently\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P14</span>More questions to practise</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 15 - What I would prioritise for a subjective exam\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1s-s15\" title=\"Part 15 - What I would prioritise for a subjective exam\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P15</span>What to prioritise</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 16 - The ideal structure for a 10-mark ERM answer\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1s-s16\" title=\"Part 16 - The ideal structure for a 10-mark ERM answer\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P16</span>Structure of a 10-mark answer</button></li><li data-full=\"Final revision - the logic connecting everything\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1s-s17\" title=\"Final revision - the logic connecting everything\">Final revision</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l1\" open><summary><div><strong>Complete Topic 1 subjective notes</strong><small>Every part of the subjective preparation, one dropdown per part.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>17 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><p>Chapter: Concepts of Risk and Uncertainty</p>\n<p>For your subjective examination, I want to approach Topic 1 differently from the MCQ preparation.</p>\n<p>In an MCQ, recognising the correct definition might earn you a mark. In a subjective exam, writing the definition alone may earn only a small fraction of the available marks.</p>\n<p>To score well, you need to demonstrate that you can:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Explain a risk concept and the reasoning behind it.</li>\n<li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar business situation.</li>\n<li>Analyse how a risk originates and affects the organisation.</li>\n<li>Evaluate competing risk-management responses and their trade-offs.</li>\n<li>Recommend and justify an appropriate decision.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This corresponds to progressively higher levels of Bloom's Taxonomy.</p>\n<p>I'll structure the preparation around the actual scope of Topic 1: risk and uncertainty, loss exposures, risk controls, risk classifications, diversification and enterprise risk categories.</p>\n<p>You'll receive model answers for short, medium and long subjective questions, marking guides, difficult case studies and practice questions.</p><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1s-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 1 - How to construct a high-scoring subjective answer</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Before studying individual questions, understand how examiners typically award marks.</p>\n<h3>1. The five-layer answer framework</h3>\n<p>For an applied ERM question, a reliable structure is:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Identify the concept</li>\n</ol>\n<p>State the relevant definition or risk category accurately.\n2. Explain the mechanism</p>\n<p>Explain why the risk exists and how it arises.\n3. Apply it to the scenario</p>\n<p>Refer directly to the company's circumstances and objective.\n4. Analyse the consequences</p>\n<p>Trace the likely operational, financial or strategic effects.\n5. Evaluate and justify a response</p>\n<p>Recommend an appropriate control or management decision, explaining its benefits and limitations.</p>\n<p>Not every question requires all five layers. A definition question worth 3 marks should not become a two-page essay.</p>\n<p>But for a 10-15 mark scenario question, this framework is extremely useful.</p>\n<h3>2. How much should you write?</h3>\n<p>These are suggested allocations, not an official marking scheme.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question value</th>\n<th>Recommended approach</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>2-3 marks</td>\n<td>Definition + one distinguishing feature or example</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4-5 marks</td>\n<td>Definition + explanation + relevant example</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6-8 marks</td>\n<td>Concept + comparison/application + causal explanation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10 marks</td>\n<td>Structured analysis of several relevant points, linked to objectives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15-20 marks</td>\n<td>Integrated analysis + risk interactions + prioritised recommendations + justification</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Important: More writing does not automatically mean more marks. An answer that repeats the definition five times is still giving one idea five times.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1s-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 2 - The fundamental concept of risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_three_definitions\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>The traditional and finance definitions are special cases; ERM uses the broader ISO definition.</figcaption></figure></div><h3>Question 1 - 5 marks</h3>\n<p>Define risk in the context of Enterprise Risk Management. Explain why establishing organisational objectives is essential when identifying risk.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Risk, in the context of Enterprise Risk Management, can be defined as the effect of uncertainty on organisational objectives. This effect may be positive, negative or a deviation from expected outcomes.</p>\n<p>Establishing organisational objectives is essential because risk cannot be meaningfully evaluated without identifying what the organisation intends to achieve.</p>\n<p>For example, an unexpected increase in crude-oil prices may increase operating costs for an airline, threatening its profitability objective. However, the same event may create a revenue opportunity for an oil producer.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the consequences of an uncertain event depend on the organisation's objectives and exposure.</p>\n<p>Consequently, organisations must establish clear objectives before identifying, assessing and managing relevant risks.</p>\n<p>This follows the broader risk definition used on page 8 of your lecture.</p>\n<h4>Suggested marking breakdown</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Correct definition of risk</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain positive/negative deviation</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain connection with objectives</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relevant organisational example</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Appropriate conclusion</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>Why this answer is stronger</h4>\n<p>A weak answer would be:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Risk is the possibility of experiencing loss.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is a traditional definition, but it misses the more comprehensive ERM interpretation.</p>\n<p>The stronger answer demonstrates:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Risk}=\\text{Uncertainty affecting objectives}}$$</div>\n<p>and explains why the same event can affect different organisations differently.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1s-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 3 - Risk vs uncertainty</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_uncertainty_spectrum\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Risk sits between certainty and uncertainty: the outcomes are known even if the result is not.</figcaption></figure></div><h3>Question 2 - 6 marks</h3>\n<p>Differentiate between risk and uncertainty. Provide appropriate business examples to illustrate your answer.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Risk and uncertainty are related concepts, but they differ in the degree of information available about possible outcomes.</p>\n<p>Risk refers to a situation in which potential outcomes can be identified and the likelihood of these outcomes may be estimated.</p>\n<p>For example, a bank may use historical borrower data to estimate the probability that a customer will default on a loan. Although repayment is not guaranteed, the bank can assess the risk using available information.</p>\n<p>Uncertainty, on the other hand, refers to situations where the possible outcomes, their probabilities or both are not adequately known.</p>\n<p>For example, a company introducing a completely new technology may struggle to anticipate consumer behaviour, future regulations and unexpected market developments because historical information is limited.</p>\n<p>The distinction is important because measurable risks are generally more suitable for quantitative assessment, whereas conditions involving substantial uncertainty may require scenario analysis, expert judgement and adaptive decision-making.</p>\n<p>Therefore, although risk and uncertainty both concern the future, risk is generally more structured and measurable than uncertainty.</p>\n<p>The lecture introduces the distinction using knowledge of possible outcomes and their odds.</p>\n<h4>Comparison table for revision</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>Risk</th>\n<th>Uncertainty</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Possible outcomes</td>\n<td>Relatively identifiable</td>\n<td>May be difficult to identify</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Probability</td>\n<td>May be estimated</td>\n<td>Often difficult to estimate reliably</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Measurement</td>\n<td>More feasible</td>\n<td>More challenging</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Example</td>\n<td>Historical loan-default exposure</td>\n<td>Disruptive new technology</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Management approach</td>\n<td>Quantitative assessment where appropriate</td>\n<td>Scenarios, judgement and adaptation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>Higher-order insight</h4>\n<p>Suppose a company estimates:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(\\text{machine failure})=5\\\\%$$</div>\n<p>That is a quantifiable risk estimate.</p>\n<p>However, suppose the company faces an entirely new regulatory environment with no meaningful historical precedent.</p>\n<p>Assigning a precise probability such as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(\\text{regulatory disruption})=17.43\\\\%$$</div>\n<p>would not necessarily make the estimate scientifically credible.</p>\n<p>Precision is not the same as accuracy.</p>\n<p>An advanced subjective answer should recognise the limitations of available information rather than pretending that every uncertainty can be reliably quantified.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1s-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 4 - Loss exposure, frequency and severity</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t1_risk_matrix\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Frequency x severity</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Set how often and how badly a loss could strike; the matrix suggests where management attention and which control belong.</figcaption></figure></div><h3>Question 3 - 8 marks</h3>\n<p>Define loss exposure. Explain how loss frequency and loss severity are used to assess risks, using a manufacturing company as an example.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Loss exposure refers to any situation or circumstance in which a loss is possible, regardless of whether an actual loss occurs.</p>\n<p>For example, a manufacturing facility that stores flammable chemicals faces fire-related loss exposure even if it has never experienced a fire.</p>\n<p>Risk managers commonly assess such exposures using two important dimensions: loss frequency and loss severity.</p>\n<p>Loss frequency refers to the probable number of losses occurring within a specified period. For example, a factory may experience several minor workplace accidents annually.</p>\n<p>Loss severity refers to the probable magnitude or financial impact of each loss. A major factory fire may be infrequent but could cause significant damage to equipment, inventory and production facilities.</p>\n<p>Both dimensions are necessary because a frequent low-severity event and an infrequent catastrophic event require different management responses.</p>\n<p>For minor recurring accidents, management may prioritise preventive safety training to reduce frequency.</p>\n<p>For a potentially catastrophic fire, management may install automatic sprinklers and prepare recovery arrangements to reduce severity.</p>\n<p>Therefore, evaluating both frequency and severity enables management to prioritise exposures and allocate resources more appropriately.</p>\n<p>These concepts are introduced on pages 13-14 of the lecture.</p>\n<h4>Suggested marking breakdown</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Loss exposure definition</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loss frequency explanation</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loss severity explanation</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relevant application and management implications</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Important evaluation question</h3>\n<p>Can management rely only on expected loss when comparing risks?</p>\n<p>Not always.</p>\n<p>Consider these hypothetical exposures:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Risk</th>\n<th>Frequency</th>\n<th>Severity</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A: Minor equipment defects</td>\n<td>Frequent</td>\n<td>RM1,000 per incident</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B: Major factory fire</td>\n<td>Rare</td>\n<td>RM20 million per incident</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Risk A may create predictable operating expenses.</p>\n<p>Risk B may threaten the company's survival.</p>\n<p>Even if their estimated annual expected losses were similar, the consequences of a catastrophic event could be much more serious for a financially vulnerable company.</p>\n<p>The advanced conclusion is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Expected loss is useful for comparing exposures, but management should also consider the possibility of catastrophic losses, business continuity and the organisation's financial capacity to absorb losses.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This last assessment is an analytical extension of the lecture's frequency/severity framework, not a separate formula prescribed by the slides.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1s-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 5 - Risk-control techniques</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_treatment_ladder\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Avoidance removes the exposure, prevention cuts frequency, reduction cuts severity.</figcaption></figure></div><h3>Question 4 - 9 marks</h3>\n<p>Differentiate between risk avoidance, loss prevention and loss reduction. Evaluate how each technique may be applied by a manufacturing company.</p>\n<p>This is one of the most examinable questions because the three techniques appear very similar.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Risk control refers to techniques used to reduce the frequency or severity of losses. The three principal techniques discussed in the lecture are risk avoidance, loss prevention and loss reduction.</p>\n<p>Risk avoidance involves not undertaking an activity that creates a particular loss exposure, or discontinuing an existing exposure.</p>\n<p>For example, a chemical manufacturer may decide not to produce an extremely hazardous product because the potential consequences exceed its acceptable risk level.</p>\n<p>Avoidance removes exposure arising from that specific activity. However, it may also eliminate potential business opportunities and revenue.</p>\n<p>Loss prevention involves implementing measures to reduce the probability or frequency of a loss occurring.</p>\n<p>For example, a manufacturer may introduce employee safety training, regular equipment inspection and preventive maintenance to reduce workplace accidents or machinery failures.</p>\n<p>Loss reduction involves measures that reduce the consequences or severity of a loss after an event occurs.</p>\n<p>For example, installing automatic fire sprinklers may limit physical damage if a factory fire occurs.</p>\n<p>In evaluating these techniques, management should consider the likelihood and severity of the exposure, implementation costs and the importance of the activity to business objectives.</p>\n<p>For commercially essential activities, prevention and reduction may be more appropriate than complete avoidance. However, avoidance may be justified when potential losses are unacceptable or cannot be adequately controlled.</p>\n<p>The lecture defines these three techniques on pages 15-16.</p>\n<h4>The comparison you should remember</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>Objective</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>Main limitation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Avoidance</td>\n<td>Remove the exposure</td>\n<td>Stop hazardous activity</td>\n<td>May sacrifice business opportunities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loss prevention</td>\n<td>Reduce frequency</td>\n<td>Safety training</td>\n<td>Event may still occur</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loss reduction</td>\n<td>Reduce severity</td>\n<td>Fire sprinklers</td>\n<td>Damage may still occur</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>Examiner's trap</h4>\n<p>Suppose management installs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>safety sensors that stop machinery before accidents,</li>\n<li>fire sprinklers that operate after ignition.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both are safety systems, but their intended effects differ.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Safety sensors → Loss prevention\"><span>Safety sensors</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Loss prevention</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Fire sprinklers → Loss reduction\"><span>Fire sprinklers</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Loss reduction</span></div>\n<p>Always classify the technique by its intended effect, not by the equipment's name.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1s-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 6 - Pure risk vs speculative risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_payoff\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Pure risk has no upside; speculative risk spreads across gain, no change and loss.</figcaption></figure></div><h3>Question 5 - 6 marks</h3>\n<p>Distinguish between pure risk and speculative risk. Explain why the distinction is relevant when selecting risk-management techniques.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Pure risk refers to a situation in which the possible outcomes are either loss or no loss, without an opportunity for financial gain.</p>\n<p>Examples include accidental fires, workplace injuries and natural disasters.</p>\n<p>Speculative risk, on the other hand, involves the possibility of either financial gain or financial loss.</p>\n<p>For example, a company investing in shares or expanding into a new market may generate positive returns or suffer losses.</p>\n<p>The distinction is important because the appropriate risk-management response may differ.</p>\n<p>Pure risks such as fire and accidental property damage can often be managed using loss prevention, loss reduction and insurance.</p>\n<p>Speculative risks generally require evaluation of potential rewards against possible losses. Depending on the exposure, management may use diversification, hedging or strategic analysis.</p>\n<p>Therefore, pure risks are primarily associated with loss exposures, whereas speculative risks require management to consider both opportunities and threats.</p>\n<p>The distinction and examples follow pages 18-19 of the lecture.</p>\n<h4>What makes this a good 6-mark answer?</h4>\n<p>It does not merely define the two risks. It explains why the difference matters to management.</p>\n<p>That final management implication is often what separates a basic answer from a more developed one.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1s-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 7 - Diversifiable vs non-diversifiable risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t1_diversification\"><div class=\"vis-title\">How diversification changes total portfolio risk</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Add assets and change how alike they are; firm-specific risk fades, the systematic floor stays.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is probably the most important finance-related subjective question in Topic 1.</p>\n<h3>Question 6 - 10 marks</h3>\n<p>Differentiate between diversifiable and non-diversifiable risks. Explain how diversification affects total portfolio risk and discuss the implications for investors.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Diversifiable risk, also known as unsystematic or firm-specific risk, refers to risk associated with individual companies or particular industries rather than the entire market.</p>\n<p>Examples include company-specific lawsuits, labour strikes, unsuccessful product launches and management failures.</p>\n<p>These risks can be reduced substantially through diversification because different companies may experience different positive and negative outcomes.</p>\n<p>For example, an investor holding shares in only one automobile manufacturer may suffer significant losses if the company experiences a labour strike.</p>\n<p>However, an investor holding shares across multiple unrelated industries is less dependent on any individual firm's performance.</p>\n<p>Non-diversifiable risk, also called systematic or market risk, refers to risk arising from broad economic or market factors affecting many companies simultaneously.</p>\n<p>Examples include inflation, changes in interest rates, recessions and business cycles.</p>\n<p>Unlike firm-specific risks, systematic risks cannot be eliminated simply by holding a large number of securities because many assets may respond to the same economic event.</p>\n<p>Conceptually, total portfolio risk consists of systematic and unsystematic components.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\text{Total Risk} = \\text{Systematic Risk} + \\text{Unsystematic Risk} }$$</div>\n<p>As the portfolio becomes more diversified, unsystematic risk generally decreases because firm-specific outcomes partly offset one another.</p>\n<p>However, systematic risk remains.</p>\n<p>Therefore, investors should diversify their holdings to reduce avoidable firm-specific exposure while recognising that broader market risks may still affect the portfolio.</p>\n<p>These distinctions are directly covered by the lecture's discussion of diversifiable and non-diversifiable risks and the concept of diversification.</p>\n<h3>Visualise the logic</h3>\n<h4>How diversification changes total portfolio risk</h4>\n<p>Conceptual illustration, not numerical data. The vertical gap between the declining curve and the systematic-risk floor represents the diminishing unsystematic-risk component.</p>\n<h4>Advanced evaluation point</h4>\n<p>A good answer can explain that diversification depends not only on the number of assets but also on whether their returns are sufficiently independent.</p>\n<p>Owning shares in 100 companies exposed to the same economic shock may offer less diversification benefit than owning assets with different underlying risk drivers.</p>\n<p>That is an application of the lecture's explanation that firm-specific surprises can offset one another, while market-wide surprises tend to affect many assets similarly.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1s-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 8 - Beta and compensation for risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t1_beta\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Beta and market sensitivity</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Beta scales an asset&#x27;s typical response to a market move; it measures co-movement, not how wild the stock is on its own.</figcaption></figure></div><h3>Question 7 - 6 marks</h3>\n<p>Explain the relationship between beta, systematic risk and expected return. Why is systematic risk particularly relevant to diversified investors?</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Beta is a measure of an asset's relative systematic risk, reflecting its sensitivity to broad market movements.</p>\n<p>According to the lecture, an asset with a beta greater than 1 has above-average systematic risk, while an asset with a beta below 1 has below-average systematic risk.</p>\n<p>For example, a share with a beta of 1.5 has greater systematic market sensitivity than a share with a beta of 0.7.</p>\n<p>Systematic risk is especially important to diversified investors because diversification can substantially reduce unsystematic risk but cannot eliminate market-wide risk.</p>\n<p>Consequently, under standard asset-pricing theory, investors generally require higher expected returns for exposure to greater systematic risk, assuming other relevant conditions remain unchanged.</p>\n<p>However, a higher beta does not guarantee that an investment will actually deliver a higher realised return.</p>\n<p>Therefore, beta is useful for assessing the systematic-risk component of an asset rather than measuring every source of investment risk.</p>\n<p>The lecture emphasises the role of beta and the relationship between systematic risk and expected returns on page 25.</p>\n<h4>Possible examiner trap</h4>\n<p>If asked:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Does an investment with a beta of 1.8 definitely earn a higher return than one with a beta of 0.8?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The answer is no.</p>\n<p>A higher beta is associated with a higher required or expected return in standard asset-pricing models, not a guaranteed actual return.</p>\n<p>That's a useful evaluation-level distinction.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1s-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 9 - Enterprise risk categories</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_category_finder\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Ask where the risk originates; the answer names the enterprise category.</figcaption></figure></div><h3>Question 8 - 10 marks</h3>\n<p>Identify and explain five major categories of enterprise risk. Illustrate how each may affect the achievement of organisational objectives.</p>\n<p>There are several valid combinations of risk categories. The following answer uses five categories explicitly discussed in the chapter.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Enterprise risk encompasses major risks capable of affecting an organisation's operations, financial performance, reputation and continued existence.</p>\n<p>Five important categories are:</p>\n<p>1. Operational risk</p>\n<p>Operational risk arises from failures involving internal processes, people, technology or control systems.</p>\n<p>For example, an unexpected IT-system failure may prevent a company from processing customer transactions, resulting in lost revenue and dissatisfaction.</p>\n<p>2. Credit risk</p>\n<p>Credit risk refers to the possibility that a debtor or counterparty fails to make required payments or makes them late.</p>\n<p>For example, if customers default on credit sales, the organisation may experience financial losses and reduced cash inflows.</p>\n<p>3. Liquidity risk</p>\n<p>Liquidity risk concerns an organisation's ability to obtain sufficient cash or liquid resources to meet financial obligations when required.</p>\n<p>For example, a company may own substantial property assets but struggle to pay suppliers because it cannot obtain immediate cash.</p>\n<p>4. Market risk</p>\n<p>Market risk arises from movements in market variables or the prices of financial instruments, such as interest rates, exchange rates and security prices.</p>\n<p>For example, unfavourable foreign-exchange movements may increase the cost of imported materials.</p>\n<p>5. Reputational risk</p>\n<p>Reputational risk arises when stakeholders develop negative perceptions of an organisation because their expectations have not been met.</p>\n<p>For example, a major product-safety scandal may reduce customer confidence and future sales.</p>\n<p>These risks are interconnected. An operational failure may generate customer dissatisfaction, reputational damage and financial losses.</p>\n<p>Therefore, effective enterprise risk management requires organisations to understand both individual risk categories and their potential interactions.</p>\n<p>The lecture covers these categories across pages 28-35.</p>\n<h3>Other categories you should be prepared to discuss</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Risk</th>\n<th>Central question</th>\n<th>Typical cause</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Business</td>\n<td>Can the business generate adequate profits?</td>\n<td>Competition, falling demand, increasing input costs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Financial</td>\n<td>Can the capital/cash-flow structure support obligations?</td>\n<td>Debt, cash-flow pressure, financing decisions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Management</td>\n<td>Can leadership make and execute effective decisions?</td>\n<td>Poor strategic decisions, ineffective leadership</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Resource</td>\n<td>Are necessary resources available?</td>\n<td>Worker shortages, lack of materials, cost overruns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quality</td>\n<td>Can products meet required standards?</td>\n<td>Defects, inadequate quality controls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Customer relationship</td>\n<td>Can the firm maintain customer confidence?</td>\n<td>Poor service, complaints, unmet expectations</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These are drawn from the risk descriptions in the chapter, particularly pages 21 and 33-36.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1s-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 10 - Credit risk vs liquidity risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_liquidity\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>A firm can be rich in assets and still fail to pay tomorrow&#x27;s obligation.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This distinction deserves separate preparation because it can appear in scenario-based questions.</p>\n<h3>Question 9 - 8 marks</h3>\n<p>Differentiate between credit risk and liquidity risk. Further distinguish between asset liquidity risk and funding liquidity risk.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Credit risk refers to the possibility that a borrower or counterparty fails to fulfil payment obligations, resulting in late payment or financial loss to the creditor.</p>\n<p>For example, a bank may experience credit risk when a borrower defaults on a loan.</p>\n<p>Liquidity risk, on the other hand, refers to difficulties in obtaining the cash required to fulfil financial obligations or execute transactions when necessary.</p>\n<p>Liquidity risk can be divided into two categories.</p>\n<p>Asset liquidity risk refers to difficulty purchasing or selling an asset because there are insufficient market participants or limited trading activity.</p>\n<p>For example, a company may be unable to sell specialised equipment quickly without accepting a substantial price reduction.</p>\n<p>Funding liquidity risk refers to the inability to meet short-term payment obligations as they become due.</p>\n<p>For example, a profitable company may lack sufficient immediately available cash to meet payroll or repay debt.</p>\n<p>Credit risk and liquidity risk can be interconnected. Customer defaults may reduce cash inflows and subsequently cause funding difficulties.</p>\n<p>Therefore, credit risk primarily concerns counterparty repayment, while liquidity risk concerns availability of cash or the ability to transact when needed.</p>\n<p>The lecture distinguishes credit and liquidity risks on pages 30-31.</p>\n<h4>The easiest memory technique</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Credit Risk}=\\text{They cannot pay me}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Funding Liquidity Risk}=\\text{I cannot pay now}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Asset Liquidity Risk}=\\text{I cannot sell easily}}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1s-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 11 - A complete 15-mark case study</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t1_cascade\"><div class=\"vis-title\">A risk cascade</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Step one event through the business: root risk first, then the consequences it triggers.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Now we move from explaining individual concepts to analysing and evaluating an unfamiliar situation.</p>\n<p>This is the level you should aim for.</p>\n<h3>Question 10 - 15 marks</h3>\n<p>A Malaysian manufacturing company operates a single production facility that produces electronic components.</p>\n<p>The company depends heavily on one overseas supplier for critical raw materials. Recently, severe flooding disrupted the supplier's operations, causing a shortage of materials. Production was delayed, customer orders could not be fulfilled, and several customers began expressing dissatisfaction online.</p>\n<p>Meanwhile, the company has substantial property assets but insufficient cash to meet short-term obligations.</p>\n<p>Required:</p>\n<p>(a) Identify and explain FOUR risks faced by the company. (8 marks)</p>\n<p>(b) Explain how these risks are interconnected. (3 marks)</p>\n<p>(c) Recommend TWO appropriate risk-management responses and justify them. (4 marks)</p>\n<h3>Model answer - Part (a), 8 marks</h3>\n<p>1. Resource risk</p>\n<p>The company faces resource risk because it depends heavily on a single supplier for critical raw materials.</p>\n<p>The supplier's disruption prevents the company from obtaining necessary production inputs, threatening its ability to fulfil customer orders.</p>\n<p>2. Operational risk</p>\n<p>The shortage of raw materials interrupts manufacturing activities.</p>\n<p>As the factory cannot operate at its intended capacity, production schedules are delayed, reducing operational efficiency and output.</p>\n<p>3. Reputational risk</p>\n<p>Customers become dissatisfied when orders are delayed.</p>\n<p>Negative customer feedback may weaken confidence in the company, potentially affecting future customer relationships and sales.</p>\n<p>4. Funding liquidity risk</p>\n<p>Although the company owns substantial property assets, it does not have enough immediately available cash to meet short-term financial obligations.</p>\n<p>Therefore, it faces funding liquidity risk despite having valuable assets.</p>\n<h3>Model answer - Part (b), 3 marks</h3>\n<p>The company's risks are interconnected because one event produces several consequences across the organisation.</p>\n<p>The flood causes supplier disruption, creating a shortage of raw materials.</p>\n<p>This leads to production delays, which prevent the company from fulfilling orders.</p>\n<p>Customer dissatisfaction then increases reputational exposure, while delayed revenue receipts worsen the firm's liquidity position.</p>\n<h4>Risk transmission chain</h4>\n<p>Flood disrupts supplier</p>\n<p>External event</p>\n<p>Raw materials unavailable</p>\n<p>Resource risk</p>\n<p>Manufacturing delayed</p>\n<p>Operational consequence</p>\n<p>Customer dissatisfaction</p>\n<p>Reputational risk</p>\n<p>Delayed cash inflows</p>\n<p>Funding liquidity pressure</p>\n<p>Threat to profitability and business continuity</p>\n<p>This demonstrates why evaluating only the original flood risk would underestimate the event's total organisational impact.</p>\n<h3>Model answer - Part (c), 4 marks</h3>\n<p>Recommendation 1: Diversify critical suppliers</p>\n<p>Management should identify and qualify additional suppliers rather than depending entirely on a single source.</p>\n<p>This could reduce the probability that one supplier's failure causes a complete interruption of production.</p>\n<p>However, supplier diversification may increase procurement and coordination costs, which should be evaluated against the expected reduction in disruption risk.</p>\n<p>Recommendation 2: Establish business-continuity and liquidity arrangements</p>\n<p>Management should develop contingency arrangements such as maintaining appropriate safety stocks, establishing alternative production arrangements and maintaining adequate liquid reserves or committed financing.</p>\n<p>These measures may reduce the severity of production disruptions and financial losses if another supplier failure occurs.</p>\n<p>However, they also involve holding costs, financing costs and other resource commitments.</p>\n<p>Consequently, management should select controls based on their effectiveness and cost relative to the potential losses.</p>\n<h4>Why this answer can score well</h4>\n<p>The answer does not simply identify:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Resource, operational, reputation, liquidity.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Instead, it demonstrates the full causal sequence:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Event → Exposure → Risk Consequence → Objective Affected → Management Response\"><span>Event</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Exposure</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Risk Consequence</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Objective Affected</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Management Response</span></div>\n<p>That is the difference between remembering and analysing.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1s-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 12 - A more challenging evaluation question</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h3>Question 11 - 12 marks</h3>\n<p>Two companies operate in the same industry.</p>\n<p>Company A experiences frequent minor equipment failures, each resulting in relatively small losses.</p>\n<p>Company B faces a very low probability of a catastrophic incident that could destroy its primary production facility and threaten the survival of the organisation.</p>\n<p>Both companies estimate similar average annual financial losses from their respective exposures.</p>\n<p>Evaluate whether both risks should receive the same management priority. Recommend appropriate risk-control techniques for each company.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Although both companies estimate similar average annual financial losses, their risks should not automatically receive the same management priority because their loss-frequency and loss-severity characteristics differ.</p>\n<p>Company A faces a high-frequency, relatively low-severity exposure.</p>\n<p>Repeated equipment failures may create recurring maintenance expenses, production interruptions and reduced operational efficiency.</p>\n<p>The most suitable response may involve loss prevention, such as regular equipment inspections, preventive maintenance and employee training.</p>\n<p>These measures seek to reduce the frequency of failures.</p>\n<p>Company B faces a low-frequency but extremely high-severity exposure.</p>\n<p>Although the catastrophic incident is unlikely, its consequences could include destruction of assets, prolonged operational disruption, major financial loss and possible business failure.</p>\n<p>Management should therefore place considerable emphasis on measures that reduce the severity of the incident, such as automatic protection systems, contingency arrangements and business-continuity planning.</p>\n<p>Loss prevention should also be considered where the probability of the incident can be reduced.</p>\n<p>The two exposures should not be assessed solely using average expected loss. Company B's catastrophic risk may be more important because a single event could exceed its financial capacity to recover.</p>\n<p>Consequently, prioritisation should consider loss frequency, severity, consequences for organisational objectives and the ability of each firm to absorb losses.</p>\n<h4>Higher-level conclusion</h4>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Equal expected losses do not necessarily imply equal risk-management decisions.</span></div>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Because:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The distribution of losses can differ.</li>\n<li>One exposure may threaten organisational survival.</li>\n<li>Different controls may be cost-effective.</li>\n<li>Financial resilience may differ.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These are analytical considerations extending the lecture's frequency/severity framework.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1s-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 13 - Tricky terminology that can cost marks</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_systematic_vs_systemic\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Systematic = one common shock to everything. Systemic = failure spreading through linked institutions.</figcaption></figure></div><h3>1. Systematic vs systemic risk</h3>\n<p>Your lecture discusses systematic risk and then describes cascading failure through interconnected financial institutions on page 23.</p>\n<p>There is a terminology issue worth recognising.</p>\n<p>Systematic risk conventionally refers to broad market risk that cannot be eliminated through diversification.</p>\n<p>Systemic risk refers to the possibility that failures spread through an interconnected system.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Interest-rate shocks affecting a broad market → systematic risk.</li>\n<li>Failure of interconnected financial institutions triggering cascading failures → systemic risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For questions based strictly on the lecture, follow the terminology being tested, but don't assume the two concepts are identical in broader financial theory.</p>\n<h3>2. Risk type vs consequence</h3>\n<p>Suppose a model gives poor predictions, causing borrowers to default.</p>\n<p>The defective decision model may represent operational/model risk.</p>\n<p>The resulting payment defaults represent credit losses.</p>\n<p>If the defaults reduce available cash, funding liquidity risk may follow.</p>\n<p>A strong answer distinguishes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Root Cause} \\neq \\text{Risk Event} \\neq \\text{Consequence}$$</div>\n<h3>3. Firm-specific risk can overlap with other categories</h3>\n<p>A company-specific lawsuit can be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>an unsystematic risk from a portfolio perspective,</li>\n<li>a legal exposure from an organisational perspective,</li>\n<li>a reputational exposure if stakeholders lose confidence.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The classifications are not mutually exclusive because they describe different dimensions.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1s-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 14 - Additional exam questions to practise independently</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>These are deliberately unanswered so you can test whether you can reproduce the reasoning without relying on a model answer.</p>\n<h3>Subjective practice paper</h3>\n<p>Suggested duration: 60 minutes · Total: 50 marks. The questions are original practice questions based on your Topic 1 scope, not predictions of the actual examination.</p>\n<p>Use each response field to draft an answer, then reveal what it should contain.</p>\n<p>0/6 attempted</p>\n<p>Question 1: Risk and objectives</p>\n<p>5 marks · 6 min</p>\n<p>An airline and an energy producer are both exposed to rising crude-oil prices. Explain why the same event may represent different risks for these two organisations.</p>\n<p>Hide marking points Grade answer</p>\n<p>Expected marking points</p>\n<p>Correct broad risk definition</p>\n<p>Identification of each business objective</p>\n<p>Explanation of opposing impacts</p>\n<p>Clear conclusion linking risk to objectives</p>\n<p>Question 2: Loss exposure and assessment</p>\n<p>6 marks · 7 min</p>\n<p>A company operates in an area vulnerable to earthquakes but has experienced no earthquake damage. Explain why the company still faces loss exposure and discuss how frequency and severity should influence its risk assessment.</p>\n<p>Hide marking points Grade answer</p>\n<p>Expected marking points</p>\n<p>Loss exposure definition</p>\n<p>Frequency and severity explained separately</p>\n<p>Application to earthquake exposure</p>\n<p>Implications for management priority</p>\n<p>Question 3: Diversification and beta</p>\n<p>8 marks · 10 min</p>\n<p>An investor holds shares in five companies operating in the same industry. Explain why the investor may remain exposed to both diversifiable and non-diversifiable risk. Discuss how beta relates to this decision.</p>\n<p>Hide marking points Grade answer</p>\n<p>Expected marking points</p>\n<p>Diversifiable and systematic risks distinguished</p>\n<p>Correlated industry exposures</p>\n<p>Benefit and limits of diversification</p>\n<p>Beta's relationship with systematic risk</p>\n<p>Question 4: Risk-management techniques</p>\n<p>10 marks · 12 min</p>\n<p>A chemical manufacturer is considering three options: discontinue a hazardous production line, install equipment to prevent accidents, or install emergency containment systems. Compare the techniques and evaluate which approach management should select.</p>\n<p>Hide marking points Grade answer</p>\n<p>Expected marking points</p>\n<p>Avoidance, prevention and reduction identified</p>\n<p>Effect on exposure, frequency or severity</p>\n<p>Business-specific examples</p>\n<p>Assessment of costs and opportunities</p>\n<p>Justified recommendation</p>\n<p>Question 5: Enterprise risk cascade</p>\n<p>10 marks · 12 min</p>\n<p>A retail company's payment system fails during a major sales campaign. Customers cannot make purchases, public complaints rise, and revenue declines. Identify and analyse the major risks involved, distinguishing the original failure from subsequent consequences.</p>\n<p>Hide marking points Grade answer</p>\n<p>Expected marking points</p>\n<p>Original operational failure identified</p>\n<p>Business and revenue impact</p>\n<p>Reputation risk</p>\n<p>Connection among risks</p>\n<p>Relevant risk-control recommendations</p>\n<p>Question 6: Integrated financial risk analysis</p>\n<p>11 marks · 13 min</p>\n<p>A manufacturer sells products on credit. Several major customers delay payment while exchange-rate movements increase the price of imported materials. The company has valuable assets but insufficient cash to pay its suppliers. Analyse the risks and recommend an appropriate response.</p>\n<p>Hide marking points Grade answer</p>\n<p>Expected marking points</p>\n<p>Credit risk explained</p>\n<p>Market/FX risk explained</p>\n<p>Funding liquidity risk distinguished</p>\n<p>Asset wealth versus cash availability</p>\n<p>Causal connections</p>\n<p>Prioritised management actions and limitations</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1s-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 15 - What I would prioritise for a subjective exam</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Not every section deserves equal revision time.</p>\n<p>Based on the content and depth of your slides, these are my recommended priorities, not confirmed examination weightings.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Priority</th>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>What you must be able to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Very high</td>\n<td>Risk vs uncertainty</td>\n<td>Explain the distinction and apply it to decisions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Very high</td>\n<td>Risk and organisational objectives</td>\n<td>Show how the same event can affect different objectives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Very high</td>\n<td>Loss frequency and severity</td>\n<td>Assess and prioritise exposures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Very high</td>\n<td>Avoidance, prevention and reduction</td>\n<td>Compare techniques and justify suitable controls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Very high</td>\n<td>Diversifiable vs systematic risk</td>\n<td>Explain portfolio diversification and its limitations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Enterprise risk categories</td>\n<td>Identify root causes, consequences and interactions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Credit and liquidity risk</td>\n<td>Distinguish repayment failure from lack of cash</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Pure vs speculative risk</td>\n<td>Differentiate outcomes and management approaches</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Beta</td>\n<td>Explain systematic-risk exposure and expected return</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Risk-return and time value of money</td>\n<td>Explain how they inform economic decisions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>That last topic deserves a quick reminder: page 3 introduces both risk-return relationships and the time value of money as fundamental financial decision concepts.</p>\n<p>For a short question, you should be able to explain that investors generally require compensation for bearing relevant risk, while money received today is typically worth more than the same nominal sum received later because it can be invested and because future receipt involves delay and potentially uncertainty.</p>\n<p>The lecture introduces these concepts briefly rather than developing detailed valuation formulas, so I would not make numerical time-value questions the centre of your Topic 1 revision without additional lecturer guidance.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1s-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 16 - The ideal structure for a 10-mark ERM answer</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>I would use this template during your exam whenever the question requires analysis and recommendations.</p>\n<p>Paragraph 1 - Establish the risk concept</p>\n<p>Define the relevant risk and link it to organisational objectives.</p>\n<p>Paragraph 2 - Identify the exposure and underlying cause</p>\n<p>Explain what creates the risk and why the company is vulnerable.</p>\n<p>Paragraph 3 - Analyse consequences</p>\n<p>Explain how the event could affect operations, finance, customers, reputation or business continuity.</p>\n<p>Paragraph 4 - Recommend risk controls</p>\n<p>Select a relevant control and explain how it reduces frequency, severity or exposure.</p>\n<p>Paragraph 5 - Evaluate the trade-off</p>\n<p>Discuss effectiveness, costs, limitations and why the proposed approach is appropriate.</p>\n<p>The most valuable part is often the final paragraph.</p>\n<p>Consider these two responses.</p>\n<p>Basic response:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The company should install fire sprinklers to reduce risk.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Higher-order response:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Management should install automatic fire sprinklers as a loss-reduction measure because they can limit the severity of damage after a fire occurs. However, sprinklers do not necessarily prevent ignition. Therefore, they should be complemented by suitable preventive controls, such as equipment inspections and safe chemical storage. Management should evaluate the combined cost of the controls against the potential severity of fire losses and the need to maintain business continuity.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The second answer demonstrates that you understand what the control does, what it does not do, and why it may be appropriate.</p>\n<p>That is precisely the reasoning expected at the Analyse-Evaluate levels of Bloom's Taxonomy.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1s-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Final revision - the logic connecting everything</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>1. Define the organisational objective</p>\n<p>What does the company want to achieve?</p>\n<p>2. Identify uncertainty and exposure</p>\n<p>What could happen, and why is the company vulnerable?</p>\n<p>3. Classify the relevant risk</p>\n<p>Pure/speculative, systematic/unsystematic, enterprise risk categories</p>\n<p>4. Evaluate frequency and severity</p>\n<p>How likely or frequent, and how damaging?</p>\n<p>5. Analyse organisational consequences</p>\n<p>How might one risk trigger others?</p>\n<p>6. Recommend and justify a response</p>\n<p>Avoid, prevent or reduce, considering cost and effectiveness</p>\n<p>If there is one thing I want you to master before the subjective exam, it is this:</p>\n<p>Do not merely identify a risk. Explain why it exists, how it affects an objective, what other consequences it creates, and why your recommended treatment is appropriate.</p>\n<p>A student who writes \"the company faces liquidity risk\" demonstrates recognition.</p>\n<p>A student who explains that \"customer payment delays have reduced cash inflows, creating funding liquidity pressure despite substantial fixed assets, and management should strengthen receivables collection and cash reserves to meet upcoming obligations\" demonstrates application, analysis and decision-making.</p>\n<p>That is the standard I would aim for across all of Topic 1.</p></div></details></div></details>");
